 summary: [
  ["Nature’s repeating clocks", "Events in nature such as sunrise and sunset, phases of the Moon and the seasons repeat after definite intervals. People used them to make calendars; a day was defined by the rising and setting of the Sun."],
  ["Early time-keeping devices", "Sundials use the changing shadow cast by the Sun (the Samrat Yantra in Jaipur is the largest stone sundial). Water clocks use water flowing out of or into a vessel; hourglasses use flowing sand; candle clocks use a marked burning candle. India’s Ghatika-yantra bowl sank in 24 minutes: one ghati, and 60 ghatis made a day."],
  ["The simple pendulum", "A bob hung from a rigid support by a thread. One oscillation is O → A → B → O (or A → B → A). The time for one oscillation is the time period; find it by timing 10 oscillations and dividing by 10."],
  ["Time period depends on length", "At a given place the time period of a pendulum of a given length is constant. It depends on the length of the string, not on the mass of the bob. Galileo discovered this; Huygens used it to build the pendulum clock (1656)."],
  ["Units of time", "All clocks count a process that repeats. The SI unit of time is the second (s); 60 s = 1 min and 60 min = 1 h. Write symbols in lowercase, singular, without a full stop, with a space after the number: 5 s, 10 min, 2 h."],
  ["Speed", "Speed is the distance covered in unit time: Speed = Total distance ÷ Total time. SI unit m/s; also km/h. Distance = Speed × Time and Time = Distance ÷ Speed. The value from total distance ÷ total time is the average speed."],
  ["Changing units", "To change km/h to m/s multiply by 1000 and divide by 3600 (for example 72 km/h = 20 m/s). A speedometer shows speed in km/h; an odometer shows distance in km."],
  ["Uniform and non-uniform motion", "Uniform linear motion: along a straight line at constant speed, covering equal distances in equal intervals of time (a straight distance–time graph). Non-uniform: the speed keeps changing, covering unequal distances in equal times. Uniform motion is an idealisation; most everyday motion is non-uniform."]
 ]
